---
/**
 * /privacy — what the site collects, where it goes, and what we do not do
 * with it. Plain and specific, in the site's voice: every statement here
 * maps to a mechanism in this repo (AuditForm, api/, analytics.js) or to a
 * setting on the GA4 property, and nothing here is a template sentence.
 *
 * Rewritten 2026-09-12 to match the GA4 property as configured (14-month
 * event retention, Google Signals off, user-provided data collection off,
 * ads personalization off, granular location and device data on) so the
 * property's user-data acknowledgement can be signed against it. May still
 * go to a lawyer. Two facts are not in the repo and are stated as such in
 * the copy: a retention period for audit requests, and a postal address.
 */
import '../styles/system.css';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';

const updated = 'September 12, 2026';
const gaId = 'G-MHQNGNC9YY';
---

<Layout
	title="Privacy | VIIS Partners"
	description="What viispartners.com collects when you request an audit or visit the site, where it is stored, who sees it, and how to have it deleted."
>
	<SiteHeader />

	<main id="main">
		<Section labelledby="privacy-heading">
			<Eyebrow class="kicker">Privacy</Eyebrow>
			<h1 id="privacy-heading" class="headline">What this site collects, and what we do with it.</h1>
			<p class="t-lead fg-2 measure sub">
				Short version: the audit form collects five things you type and we store them in our own
				Azure account to run your audit and reply; Google Analytics counts visits and records when a
				request is accepted, without advertising features; nothing is sold or shared with anyone.
			</p>
			<p class="t-mono fg-3 updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

			<div class="prose measure">
				<h2>The audit form</h2>
				<p>
					When you request a free audit, the form sends us your name, your business name, your
					email address, your website address, and, if you add one, a one-line note. That is all
					it asks for. Every field has a length cap, and line breaks and control characters are
					stripped before anything is stored.
				</p>
				<p>
					With those fields we record the time of the request, your browser's identifying string,
					and a hash of your network address. The address itself is never stored: it is run through
					SHA-256 and only the first half of the result is kept, which cannot be turned back into
					the address. The hash is stored with your request and counted for one hour so that a
					single source cannot flood the form; after five requests in an hour the form refuses
					until the hour is up.
				</p>
				<p>
					The form carries a hidden checkbox that a person never sees. Automated submissions that
					tick it are discarded without being stored.
				</p>
				<p>
					Two emails go out when a request is accepted: a confirmation to the address you gave,
					and a notification to us. Both contain what you typed and nothing else.
				</p>

				<h2>Where it is stored</h2>
				<p>
					Requests are written to a table in a Microsoft Azure storage account that belongs to
					VIIS, in Azure's East US 2 region. The two emails are sent through Azure Communication
					Services from the same account. No third-party form tool, mailing service, or marketing
					platform sits in that path.
				</p>
				<p>
					The hosting platform, Azure Static Web Apps, keeps its own standard operational logs. Our
					own application logs record the hostname you submitted and a prefix of the hash, never a
					name or an email address.
				</p>

				<h2>Who sees it</h2>
				<p>
					One person: Jadrin Garcia, the principal of VIIS. Your request is used to run the audit,
					write the report, and reply to you. It is not used for a newsletter, it is not added to
					any list, and it is never sold, rented, or shared with another business.
				</p>
				<p>
					We keep audit requests for as long as we need them to run the audit and follow up on
					it. Ask and we delete yours.
				</p>

				<h2>Analytics</h2>
				<p>
					The site uses Google Analytics 4, property <span class="t-mono">{gaId}</span>, to count
					visits and to record when an audit request is accepted, so we know whether the site is
					doing its job. On each page it loads it sends Google the page address, the page you came
					from, the time, your browser and operating system, your screen size, and an approximate
					location derived from your network address, down to city level. Google does not log or
					store the address itself.
				</p>
				<p>
					The property is configured so that it does not build an advertising profile of you and
					does not connect your visit to Google's ad products: Google Signals is off, so no
					cross-device tracking and no demographic or interest data; ads personalization is off;
					and user-provided data collection is off, so nothing you type on this site, including on
					the audit form, is sent to Google. The one event beyond page views, sent when a request
					is accepted, carries no form data.
				</p>
				<p>
					Google keeps the event-level data for fourteen months, then deletes it; aggregated
					reports remain. Google processes this traffic on its own servers under its own terms,
					which may place it outside the United States.
				</p>

				<h2>Cookies</h2>
				<p>
					The site sets no cookies of its own. Google Analytics sets two first-party cookies,
					<span class="t-mono">_ga</span> and <span class="t-mono">_ga_MHQNGNC9YY</span>, each
					lasting up to two years, to tell a returning visit from a new one. They are set when a
					page loads; there is no consent banner. Blocking or deleting them in your browser does
					not affect the site or the form, and with scripts disabled the analytics never loads at
					all.
				</p>

				<h2>What we do not do</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>We do not sell, rent, trade, or share your details with anyone.</li>
					<li>We do not send marketing email. A request gets a confirmation and a reply, and that is all.</li>
					<li>We do not embed social media, advertising pixels, chat widgets, or any third-party script other than Google Analytics.</li>
					<li>We do not collect anything from you that the form does not show you.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Your choices</h2>
				<p>
					Email us and we will send you a copy of what we hold about you, correct it, or delete
					it. There is no form for this and no waiting period: it is a reply from a person. For
					analytics, block the two cookies above or use Google's opt-out browser add-on; nothing on
					the site depends on them.
				</p>

				<h2>Contact</h2>
				<p>VIIS, LLC (d/b/a VIIS Partners), San Antonio, Texas.</p>
				<p><a href="mailto:jgarcia@viispartners.com">jgarcia@viispartners.com</a></p>
			</div>
		</Section>
	</main>

	<SiteFooter />
</Layout>

<style>
	.kicker {
		display: flex;
	}

	.headline {
		margin-top: var(--sp-5);
	}

	.sub {
		margin-top: var(--sp-5);
	}

	.updated {
		margin-top: var(--sp-5);
	}

	.prose {
		margin-top: var(--sp-8);
	}

	/* Sections within the policy: an h2 opens each, with a section's worth
	   of breath above it but the prose rhythm below. */
	.prose h2 {
		margin-top: var(--sp-8);
	}

	.prose h2:first-child {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.prose h2 + * {
		margin-top: var(--sp-4);
	}

	.prose ul > li {
		position: relative;
		padding-left: var(--sp-5);
	}

	.prose ul > li + li {
		margin-top: var(--sp-3);
	}

	/* The site's jade mark, on the item's first line. */
	.prose ul > li::before {
		content: '';
		position: absolute;
		left: 0;
		top: calc((var(--text-body) * var(--lh-body) - 6px) / 2);
		width: 6px;
		height: 6px;
		border-radius: 50%;
		background: var(--accent);
	}
</style>
